{"hash":"95a95e11192d4c47","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":92.704,"said":"You are at the river’s edge. Look at the pilgrims around you. Some have come from Tamil Nadu, from the other end of India, and they carry something special: a little sand from the shore at Rameshwaram, where Rama worshipped Shiva beside the sea. Tradition says the sand is given here, to the Ganga, with prayers. Then the pilgrim fills a vessel with Ganga water, seals it, and carries it all the way back south, to pour over Ramanathaswamy in abhishekam. Only then, by tradition, is the yatra complete. So Kashi in the north and Rameshwaram in the south are tied together by one journey: sand going north, water going south. Now look to your left, downstream. There smoke rises from Manikarnika, the burning ghat, where, it is said, the fires never go out. Kashi believes that whoever dies here is freed from rebirth, for at the last moment Shiva himself whispers into their ear the Taraka mantra, the mantra that carries one across. And to your right, at Dashashwamedh Ghat, priests raise great flaming lamps to the river every evening in the Ganga aarti. Now dip your hands, and climb back up with me. Nandi is waiting beside the well of wisdom.\n\nDid you know? In July 2025 the two temples began a formal exchange: urns of holy water and sand travelled between Kashi Vishwanath and Rameshwaram for the abhishekam of both Lords.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1147},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1163,"end":1329}],"words":[[0.098,0.24,0,3],[0.35,0.264,4,7],[0.627,0.18,8,10],[0.819,0.084,11,14],[0.915,0.517,15,22],[1.444,0.349,23,27],[2.704,0.252,29,33],[2.969,0.096,34,36],[3.077,0.084,37,40],[3.173,0.529,41,49],[3.714,0.397,50,56],[4.123,0.24,57,60],[5.228,0.288,62,66],[5.529,0.228,67,71],[5.769,0.24,72,76],[6.022,0.228,77,81],[6.262,0.337,82,87],[6.611,0.433,88,92],[7.272,0.216,94,98],[7.5,0.168,99,102],[7.68,0.373,103,108],[8.065,0.168,109,112],[8.245,0.156,113,115],[8.413,0.4